35009765625, -0.006427764892578125, -0.031280517578125, 0.0007987022399902344, 0.017852783203125, -0.0008420944213867188, 0.0166473388671875, 0.01401519775390625, -0.017486572265625, -0.012542724609375, 0.029754638671875, 0.0037670135498046875, 0.043243408203125, 0.0109710693359375, 0.0091705322265625, -0.006256103515625, 0.040740966796875, 0.0116424560546875, -0.00251007080078125, 0.01155853271484375, -0.0287933349609375, -0.0114593505859375, 0.00875091552734375, -0.0099334716796875, -0.0200347900390625, 0.033477783203125, -0.005706787109375, 0.041107177734375, -0.01727294921875, 0.0072479248046875, -0.0014467239379882812, -0.0027179718017578125, 0.0179290771484375, -0.01412200927734375, 0.0028247833251953125, -0.01776123046875, -0.0223846435546875, 0.044158935546875, -0.0222625732421875, -0.01229095458984375, 0.01165771484375, -0.00983428955078125, 0.01544952392578125, 0.00689697265625, 0.040283203125, 0.031707763671875, -0.0050201416015625, -0.004169464111328125, -0.002117156982421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nIntroduction: Structured radiology reporting is essential for clear communication and quality assurance, yet variability in report formats and content can hinder consistency and patient care. Automated tools that standardize report structure and flag quality issues may improve reporting practices. This study aims to address these challenges by developing and evaluating a multi-agent AI system for structuring and quality assurance of radiology reports.\n\nMaterials and Methods:\n\nDataset: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024 at a single institution. Reports were selected consecutively from routine clinical practice without exclusion criteria. The use of reports from only 15 radiologists may introduce bias related to individual reporting styles and institutional practices.\n\nAI System Development: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system was developed, consisting of specialized agents with distinct roles. The structuring agent segmented reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level. This was achieved using pattern-matching rules (regular expressions) to identify anatomical terms and section headers, combined with locally run large language models\u2014AI models trained on large text datasets but deployed within the institution\u2014to ensure accurate classification. The system included several QA agents: (1) a section mismatch agent that detected inconsistencies between the Findings and Impression sections, (2) an intra-section consistency agent that identified contradictions within sections, (3) a gender-anatomy conflict agent that flagged gender-specific anatomical inconsistencies, and (4) a critical findings agent that checked for documentation of communication regarding critical findings. Agents operated in sequence, with the output of the structuring agent serving as input for the QA agents, and each agent\u2019s output was passed to the next for further analysis.\n\nEvaluation Process: The evaluation phase was conducted separately from system development. Two board-certified radiologists independently evaluated a subset of 45 reports that had been restructured and flagged by the system. Each radiologist reviewed the reports using the following criteria: (1) accuracy of anatomical structuring (correct assignment of sentences to anatomical sections), (2) retention of original clinical content (no loss of clinically relevant information), (3) absence of fabricated information (no addition of content not present in the original report), and (4) overall QA performance. QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" (no errors and all issues correctly flagged), \"good\" (minor errors or missed flags not affecting clinical interpretation), or \"fair\" (significant errors or missed issues that could impact clinical care). Disagreements were resolved by discussion, and the nature of disagreements was recorded to identify potential system limitations. Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic.\n\nResults: The multi-agent AI system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content. The system flagged 90 reports (14.1% of 638), most commonly for section mismatches (80 reports, 12.5%). In the radiologist evaluation of 45 reports, both reviewers agreed that 31 (69%) were correctly restructured, 2 (4%) were incorrectly restructured, and disagreed on 12 (27%), yielding a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.62, indicating substantial agreement. Disagreements primarily involved borderline cases of anatomical section assignment (e.g., sentences describing findings at anatomical boundaries such as the diaphragm) and interpretation of ambiguous sentences (e.g., statements like \"no acute abnormality\" without clear anatomical reference). These cases were resolved through consensus discussion, with reviewers considering clinical context and standard reporting conventions. Both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced. Overall QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" in 28 reports (62%), \"good\" in 10 reports (22%), and \"fair\" in 7 reports (16%).\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system combined radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow, demonstrating favorable performance in radiologist evaluation. The retrospective design and use of a single-institution dataset may limit generalizability, as results could be influenced by local reporting practices and the small number of radiologists. To mitigate these limitations, future work should include prospective validation, expansion to multi-institutional datasets to increase diversity and reduce bias, and recruitment of a larger and more varied group of radiologists. Further, agent collaboration can be refined by implementing consensus-based rules for anatomical section assignment and incorporating advanced natural language processing techniques to better handle ambiguous language. Such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice."}}